      "standard": "Vitest rules: assert throws through the matcher (`expect(() => fn()).toThrow(...)`), not via try/catch",
      "severity": "follow_up",
      "summary": "The unknown-code tests capture the error with a hand-written try/catch instead of `expect(() => couponDiscount('SAVE99', 2000)).toThrow(UnknownCouponError)` plus a `toThrow(/SAVE99/)` check; the same pattern is repeated in test/pricing.test.js:67.",
      "failureScenario": "The assertions sit outside the catch, so a call that doesn't throw still fails (`error` is undefined and `toBeInstanceOf` fails). The tests prove the right thing. The problem is that if the call stops throwing, the failure reads 'expected undefined to be an instance of UnknownCouponError' rather than Vitest's 'expected function to throw', and the try/catch is the pattern the Vitest skill lists as an anti-pattern. This is a cleanup, not a correctness issue.",
      "id": "R1.1",
      "round": 1,
      "status": "follow_up"
    }
